      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 4,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Exercise: video AR on a planar marker - one foreach body, not N copies\n\nA handheld 9x6 chessboard clip (LoadVideo -> Video Slice -> GetVideoComponents = an N-frame IMAGE batch).\nDETECT ONCE (frame 0): findChessboardCorners + 'CV Grid Points' + 'CV Camera Matrix' -> 'Solve PnP (Pose)'. The object grid REPROJECTED through that pose (cv2.projectPoints) is the reference marker (always a valid array; raw findChessboardCorners returns None with no board -> would crash the transform below). Frame 0's own cube overlay seeds the output batch.\nTRACK THE REST (frames 1..N-1): one ImageFromBatch pulls them as a LIST (batch_index = a 'range'), then Inspire foreach runs a SINGLE body per frame: 'Track Features (KLT)' from the reference -> 'Find Homography (RANSAC)' (plane; RANSAC drops off-plane tracks) -> cv2.perspectiveTransform relocates the known marker corners -> 'Solve PnP (Pose)' + cv2.projectPoints draw the cube -> gate on 'reference found AND homography found' -> APPEND to the running batch ('Image Batch' = the fold accumulator). 'ForeachListEnd' yields the whole overlaid batch -> 'Preview Image' (the AR video).\n\nWhy foreach fits here (and not in visual odometry): each frame is tracked INDEPENDENTLY from the reference keyframe, so the loop carries ONE accumulator (the output frames) - no second running state (a trajectory) to collect. Tracking from the reference avoids drift; a long clip with large motion would instead track frame-to-frame and compose the per-step homographies (cv2.gemm), periodically re-anchored by a fresh detection."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
